 like  what's this day so anyway thanks for  choosing to spend a weekend afternoon  inside watching this movie and sticking  around to chat with us deeply  appreciated  um  theater to film yes  um and you're right I have done some TV  although um before we started shooting  this film  we started photography for this movie in  the summer of 2019 we would then have an  interruption and have to wait a couple  of years what was that Interruption oh  well there were a few  um uh one was an interruption a lot of  other people had to navigate as well one  was not  um I mean that story essentially  in brief  is that we shot this film where it's set  in New Orleans which as you probably  know is a very vulnerable place to climb  it  um and in that first summer uh we we  moved through the flash floods and the  heat waves and the lightning holds but  there was a hurricane that stopped us in  our tracks and we had to evacuate and  there was a Jen had a heart out  um for an important event so um we  always knew we would be coming back when  we lost that time for the hurricane and  then by the time we had gotten everybody  together it was March of 2020 and I  think about 18 hours before I was meant  to get on a plane I got a call that no  one would be shooting  um that March or for many months after  how did that feel after that wait I mean  the emotional journey of coming to that  point that must have been devastating  um it was  um I will also say that at that point I  mean I would then have to relive that  moment about three times over in the  course of the pandemic I think at that  point it was devastating  it was also already contextualized by  what was beginning to unfold globally  and so I think the context though we had  so little understanding then forgive me  I'm like how long we don't have to dwell  on the pandemic too long in this  TalkBack but um I think there was there  was a lot going on uh I would say the  having the wind knocked out of your  sails is someone it would be an  understatement for how I felt personally  in that moment but there was also a lot  going on in the world  um and then essentially we would um  try to begin again and pause and  ultimately we're able to withstand the  challenges of the pandemic and get  everyone back together the summer of  2021 so two years later  um but to your question sorry that was  that was my way of saying 2019 at that  point I think I had spent three days on  the television set prior to starting to  shoot this film  um but yes my life has primarily been as  a theater director  um and I mean part of the joys of doing  this for the first time is  um was being in touch with the Deep  commonality in the core practice uh in  in ways that are probably very apparent  to you in terms of the centrality of the  conversation with the actor the Primacy  of visual composition the core  storytelling impulses the processes are  of course structured in radically  different ways  and so on a very I think basic level  um the the difference is the endurance  involved in feature filmmaking  um when you open a play  um that's when my contract ends  um I say goodbye and the actors continue  to carry the story every night  um and I think the the  being the constant the through line  across a changing landscape of  collaborators and creative processes  carrying it with you every single day  um that is the responsibility the thrill  and the resilience involved in that I  think was edifying  and Lila you intimated a little bit  about your experience working with the  actors here but I'd love to know how  much time you had with Jennifer and  Brian who's so wonderful in this as he  always is before you shot were you able  to Workshop this in an intimate setting  did they do work on their own and come  back to you how did that work  um  well  Brian with Brian the foundation is that  I've known Brian since I was 19.  yeah so I was in undergrad when he was  in drama school  so Brian is an old friend and I first