rked with specialists in this  space to make sure you were telling this  authentically what did you learn even  just as a citizen of this country about  what people endure that we never  know about that we never see and someone  specifically like Lindsay  I think this is something Lila should  chime in on certainly but before you do  I think  we learned more than I could possibly  distill on this stage right now I think  one of the primary things we learned is  that recovery looks different for  everybody and that trauma can often be  quiet the way we carry it can be  internal  I think we haven't seen a lot of films  where it is demonstrated in that way we  see the physical you know maybe you know  Prosthetics or whatever injuries but we  don't see what's beyond that yes yes so  in a way it sort of felt like maybe  where we were subverting the tropes of  that genre  um but in terms of the magnitude of the  research that was done I think you  should chime in on that  um  I suspect that Justine is is cueing me  because no because she knows and please  forgive me if this sounds deeply cheesy  or hyperbolic but because  um the opportunity I had while working  on this film to consult at Great length  with veterans and U.S armed forces  service members and medical  professionals from the VA both in New  York and in New Orleans those  conversations were life-changing for me  um and the film wouldn't exist without  those people  um what was their tone when they found  out you were telling this story was it  one of gratitude was it one of fear  hoping you would cast it you know in the  right way  I mean  um in on any subject when talking about  the wide-ranging conversations I've had  I will confess I'm reticent to um to  sort of to forgive me to generalize  about people's opinions because I think  part of what was revelatory for me was  um beginning to recognize the various  assumptions that I've made as a civilian  about the kind of people that enlist  um uh and I think the opportunity to  have those assumptions challenged and  upended and to recognize that population  is not monolithic at all  um among various aspects  um uh  but forgive me I do want to answer your  question  no because I actually think it was all  of the above like I love to ask the  question um what do they get wrong in  the movies uh and sometimes it's  actually kind of like wildly wonderfully  pedestrian things actually like about  how certain badges are worn sometimes it  was significantly bigger things and of  course those answers were personal and  specific but um I think there was both  and I felt very humbled by this I think  there was gratitude and I would say  um tentative excitement particularly  among the veterans I spoke to had who  had incurred tbis  because that is really um it has become  the Hallmark injury of the Contemporary  military because of how pervasive IEDs  are  um so there was I think  trepidatious excitement about seeing  that condition represented and and I'm  sure anxiety about getting it wrong  um and as we see at the beginning the  recovery is different for every patient  there isn't one way that we expect okay  after six weeks you're ready to go back  and we and I love that we see that  struggle at the beginning with her nurse  saying you're not ready  um 100 yes and I think again while it's  hard to distill per per where you  started in terms of I think what some of  the takeaways were I think you said  something to this effect but in part  that  um and I think you can globalize this  beyond the military experience but that  recovery is rarely linear making what we  might call progress in any way is rarely  linear  um and also that  likewise progress and Recovery is often  charted in what can look like incredibly  small steps  um hard thought  very difficult steps but that to someone  standing even this close you know might  look this big  I wish we could talk all day because I  feel like we're just getting into some  juicy stuff 